[2026-03-14T20:52:23.164Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017Sn1CzXmgEoWZccH1NRkMK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me check the host-stats function more carefully. For the \"zeros\" test, Emma has no properties, so the host-stats queries should return 0 for everything. But wait - the auth system stores the user in Redux. When we make Emma a host via API, the frontend Redux state won't know about it. When she navigates to /hosting, the HostDashboard checks `currentUser.is_host`. If the login stored the old is_host=false value, it would show the upgrade prompt, not the dashboard.\n\nI need to think about this. The login response likely includes is_host. Let me check the auth function.\n\n\nActually, I see the issue now - logging in sets is_host=false in Redux, then the API call updates the database but not the frontend state. So navigating to /hosting would still show the upgrade prompt since Redux hasn't been refreshed. I could register a new user with is_host=true from the start, reload the page after the API call to re-fetch user data, or just use the \"Become a Host\" button on the profile page. The third option seems most straightforward.\n\nBut I'm realizing I need to understand how the profile page works and whether the app persists auth state to localStorage on reload. Looking at the seed data, the database resets between tests anyway, so maybe the cleanest approach is to just use a different user - I could login as Mike who's already a host and delete his properties before the test.\n\nActually, I'm checking the host-stats query now. When I deactivate properties by setting is_active=false, the totalListings count drops to zero since it filters by is_active. But the activeBookings query only checks the host_id and booking status - it doesn't filter by property is_active. So even with both properties deactivated, Mike would still show 1 active booking from the existing pending booking.\n\nTo get true zeros across the board, I need a host with no properties at all. That means creating a fresh host user, but I'm realizing the auth system doesn't have a separate create-user endpoint - registration and authentication are tied together. Let me check what the auth function actually returns to see if I can work with that.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":735,"cache_read_input_tokens":74670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":735,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b502c6d-2fab-4348-89e8-3447774cafbf","uuid":"9248f8ac-7d91-42b3-9466-1278008df1b6"}
